The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (data == NULL) {
    xfer->status = USB_ERR_IOERROR;
    UPRINTF(LFTL, "%s unexpected NULL data\r\n", __func__);
    goto out;
}
```
Reason:
The code incorrectly treats a `NULL` data pointer as an error without considering the case where the length (`len`) is `0`. This leads to erroneous error reporting during USB enumeration when `data` is `NULL` but `len` is `0`, which is a valid scenario according to the USB specification.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
/* (data == NULL && len == 0) is okey to libusb_control_transfer */
if (data == NULL && len) {
    xfer->status = USB_ERR_IOERROR;
    UPRINTF(LFTL, "%s unexpected NULL data\r\n", __func__);
    goto out;
}
```
Reason:
The fix introduces a condition that allows the function to proceed without error when `data` is `NULL` and `len` is `0`, which aligns with the USB specification that permits control transfers without a data stage in this case. This prevents unnecessary I/O errors during USB enumeration.

